# XAI Explainability & Guardrail Evaluation Toolbox

A compact, model-agnostic audit package for checking binary classification pipelines before deployment. It reports overall predictive quality, subgroup fairness, probability calibration, configurable guardrail decisions, and model-agnostic local feature attributions.

## Quick start

```powershell
py -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -e ".[plots,test]"
python -m xai_guardrails.cli --output artifacts
```

The demo trains a small scikit-learn classifier on generated data and writes `artifacts/audit_report.json` and `artifacts/group_metrics.png`. The Python module command works from a source checkout; the `xai-guardrails` command is available after installation.

## Audit a model

Pass ground-truth labels, positive-class scores, and sensitive-group labels to the same audit function. Scores can come from scikit-learn `predict_proba` or Keras `model.predict`; this package does not require a particular model framework.

```python
from xai_guardrails import AuditThresholds, audit_predictions

report = audit_predictions(
    y_true=y_test,
    y_score=model.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1],
    sensitive_features=protected_group,
    thresholds=AuditThresholds(max_demographic_parity_gap=0.10, max_ece=0.08),
)
print(report.to_dict())
```

For a Keras sigmoid output, pass `model.predict(X_test, verbose=0).ravel()` as `y_score`. For a two-column softmax output, pass the positive-class column.

## What is measured

- Overall accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC AUC, Brier score, and expected calibration error (ECE).
- Per-group support, selection rate, accuracy, true-positive rate, and false-positive rate.
- Demographic-parity gap, equal-opportunity gap, equalized-odds gap, and selection-rate ratio.
- Configurable pass/fail guardrails with the observed value and threshold in every finding.
- Model-agnostic local feature attributions based on replacing one input feature with a reference value and measuring the change in positive-class score.

The fairness statistics are descriptive diagnostics, not proof that a system is fair. They depend on representative labels, adequately sized groups, legally and ethically appropriate sensitive attributes, and context-specific thresholds. The toolbox does not infer protected classes, make deployment decisions, or replace a domain/legal review. Small groups and missing subgroups are explicitly surfaced rather than silently treated as passing.

## Explain an individual prediction

`local_feature_attributions` works with a prediction callback that returns positive-class probabilities. Numeric columns use the supplied reference row; if none is provided, the function uses the column median. It handles tabular NumPy arrays and pandas-like frames while preserving feature names when available. The optional `explain` extra installs SHAP for projects that need a model-specific explainer; the core package has no SHAP or TensorFlow dependency.

## EthicalML / Fairlearn integration

The audit report uses explicit, framework-neutral definitions so it can sit around EthicalML, Fairlearn, scikit-learn, or Keras pipelines without changing model code. Keep external fairness-toolkit metrics alongside this report when a project has standardized on a particular toolkit, and compare definitions before interpreting results; similarly named metrics can use different denominators or undefined-group policies.

